6. LOCAL DATA ARCHITECTURE

Recommendation: use Room, which sits on SQLite and provides typed queries,
relationships, transactions, and migrations. An in-memory dictionary alone
would lose changes when the app closes.

Proposed structure:

  domain/model/
    Product.kt
    Vendor.kt
    Transaction.kt
    TransactionItem.kt
    StockMovement.kt
    PendingChange.kt

  data/local/
    PanindaDatabase.kt
    entities/
    dao/
    DemoData.kt

  data/repository/
    ShopRepository.kt

  adapters/sheets/
    WorkbookSchema.kt
    SheetsMapper.kt
    GoogleSheetsAdapter.kt

  adapters/customdb/

  ui/
    home/HomeScreen.kt
    sell/SellScreen.kt
    inventory/InventoryScreen.kt
    history/HistoryScreen.kt
    tools/ToolsScreen.kt
    sync/SyncStatusScreen.kt
    settings/SettingsScreen.kt
    components/

Every additional page/subpage gets a separate Kotlin file. Screens receive
data through ViewModels and the shared repository.

Shared records should use stable IDs, unique SKUs, integer money amounts,
timestamps, and transaction-item snapshots. Later product edits must not
change historical receipt names, prices, or vendor attribution.

7. ADAPTER BOUNDARIES

Google Sheets remains a required future integration, implemented as an
adapter. Product, inventory, and transaction rules remain independent of
workbook cells. Custom database support is another adapter and remains disabled.

The Sheets adapter owns:
  - Tab names and exact headers starting at A1.
  - Schema version and field validation.
  - Conversion between shared records and spreadsheet rows.
  - Future upload, download, and conflict handling.

Map structured records to rows. Arbitrary SQL-to-spreadsheet-formula translation
is not required.

The workbook schema and mapping logic can be implemented and tested locally.
Actual Google authentication, workbook selection, synchronization, retry
processing, and multi-device conflict resolution remain pending for this phase.
Offline messages must accurately state that cloud sync is not implemented yet.

8. STRING ORGANIZATION

Use page-specific resource files, for example:
  res/values/strings_home.xml
  res/values/strings_inventory.xml
  res/values/strings_settings.xml

Android combines these into one resource namespace. Use page prefixes such as
home_today_sales to avoid collisions.

9. IMPLEMENTATION STATUS DOCUMENTATION

If implementation proceeds, document implemented, demo-only, and pending
behavior in docs/PROTOTYPE_STATUS.md, including:
  - Every unfinished button and workflow.
  - Missing screens and design-derived additions.
  - Demo data versus persistent user-entered records.
  - Adapter and synchronization limitations.
  - Disconnect and reset semantics.

Reports, vendor management, and selling functionality are explicitly pending.
Stock counting is recommended as pending because its workflow is not defined.

10. FEASIBILITY AND VERIFICATION

Feasible now:
  - Responsive Compose screens and reusable component variants.
  - Fixed headers/search/filter controls and isolated scrolling regions.
  - Navigation, context-specific bottom-sheet filters, and icon-bearing FABs.
  - Room-backed local persistence and consistent derived dashboard totals.
  - Local workbook schema/mapping tests without a Google connection.
  - Build, lint, data tests, and rendered phone-size layout checks.

Outside this prototype phase:
  - Actual Google authentication, Drive selection, and cloud synchronization.
  - Multi-device synchronization/conflict resolution.
  - Selling, payment, vendor-management, and reporting workflows.
  - Automatic Figma Code Connect mapping with the current seat.

Actual-device acceptance testing remains necessary for device-specific behavior.

11. PROPOSED BRANCH

